1
00:00:00,361 --> 00:00:02,025
Last time, we saw that the rows and

2
00:00:02,105 --> 00:00:04,972
columns of the periodic table follow electron shells.

3
00:00:05,736 --> 00:00:07,209
Here's a puzzle from that table.

4
00:00:07,917 --> 00:00:10,433
Sodium and chlorine sit in the same row.

5
00:00:11,139 --> 00:00:13,404
A chlorine atom has six more protons and

6
00:00:13,485 --> 00:00:15,550
six more electrons than a sodium atom.

7
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:17,350
So you'd expect it to be bigger.

8
00:00:18,165 --> 00:00:18,550
It's not.

9
00:00:19,057 --> 00:00:20,844
Chlorine's atom is much smaller.

10
00:00:21,528 --> 00:00:23,358
And go down a column, from lithium to

11
00:00:23,399 --> 00:00:25,230
potassium, and atoms get larger.

12
00:00:25,993 --> 00:00:28,539
So why does atomic radius change across a

13
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:29,983
period and down a group?

14
00:00:30,961 --> 00:00:33,526
An atom has no hard edge, so chemists

15
00:00:33,606 --> 00:00:35,710
measure the distance between the nuclei of two

16
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:38,736
identical atoms bonded together, and take half.

17
00:00:39,398 --> 00:00:40,544
That's the atomic radius.

18
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:43,105
It's tiny, so we use picometres.

19
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:45,904
A picometre is a millionth of a millionth

20
00:00:46,006 --> 00:00:46,428
of a metre.

21
00:00:47,173 --> 00:00:49,062
Every value we compare today is measured the

22
00:00:49,103 --> 00:00:51,353
same way, so the comparison is fair.

23
00:00:52,542 --> 00:00:54,990
Two things decide how close the outer electrons

24
00:00:55,030 --> 00:00:55,231
sit.

25
00:00:55,814 --> 00:00:57,020
The first is the nucleus.

26
00:00:57,604 --> 00:01:00,911
Its positive protons attract the negative electrons, and

27
00:01:00,952 --> 00:01:02,234
more protons pull harder.

28
00:01:02,938 --> 00:01:04,003
The second is shielding.

29
00:01:04,688 --> 00:01:06,954
Inner electrons sit between the nucleus and the

30
00:01:07,116 --> 00:01:09,785
outer electrons, and their repulsion cancels part of

31
00:01:09,805 --> 00:01:10,768
the nucleus's pull.

32
00:01:11,451 --> 00:01:12,973
A simple way to estimate the pull an

33
00:01:13,074 --> 00:01:16,019
outer electron feels is the core charge: the

34
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:18,463
number of protons, minus the number of inner

35
00:01:18,544 --> 00:01:19,024
electrons.

36
00:01:19,767 --> 00:01:22,555
For sodium, that's eleven minus ten, so plus

37
00:01:22,676 --> 00:01:22,816
one.

38
00:01:23,419 --> 00:01:25,792
Exam answers call this net pull the effective

39
00:01:25,953 --> 00:01:26,737
nuclear charge.

40
00:01:27,455 --> 00:01:30,062
Now go across Period three, from sodium to

41
00:01:30,103 --> 00:01:30,523
chlorine.

42
00:01:31,308 --> 00:01:33,703
Each step adds one proton to the nucleus.

43
00:01:34,368 --> 00:01:36,491
The new electron goes into the same third

44
00:01:36,552 --> 00:01:39,437
shell, so the inner shells don't change, and

45
00:01:39,458 --> 00:01:41,261
there are still ten inner electrons.

46
00:01:42,043 --> 00:01:44,834
So the core charge climbs, from plus one

47
00:01:44,875 --> 00:01:47,124
for sodium to plus seven for chlorine.

48
00:01:47,888 --> 00:01:50,256
A stronger pull on the same shell draws

49
00:01:50,298 --> 00:01:52,767
the outer electrons closer, and the atom gets

50
00:01:52,828 --> 00:01:53,188
smaller.

51
00:01:54,077 --> 00:01:56,151
Hang on, chlorine has more electrons.

52
00:01:56,596 --> 00:01:57,632
Shouldn't they take up more room?

53
00:01:58,377 --> 00:02:00,540
The extra electrons all go into the same

54
00:02:00,742 --> 00:02:01,222
outer shell,

55
00:02:01,744 --> 00:02:03,947
and electrons in the same shell barely shield

56
00:02:03,988 --> 00:02:04,308
each other.

57
00:02:04,790 --> 00:02:07,097
So the nucleus's extra pull wins.

58
00:02:07,881 --> 00:02:10,234
More electrons in the same shell doesn't mean

59
00:02:10,356 --> 00:02:11,059
a bigger atom.

60
00:02:11,801 --> 00:02:12,391
Quick prediction.

61
00:02:12,937 --> 00:02:15,627
Lithium and potassium are both in Group one.

62
00:02:16,190 --> 00:02:18,973
Potassium has sixteen more protons pulling on its

63
00:02:19,014 --> 00:02:19,473
electrons.

64
00:02:20,056 --> 00:02:21,370
So which atom is larger?

65
00:02:21,958 --> 00:02:22,363
Have a think.

66
00:02:25,997 --> 00:02:26,602
Potassium.

67
00:02:26,965 --> 00:02:29,552
Going down Group one, each element has one

68
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:31,577
more occupied shell than the one above,

69
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:34,265
so its outer electron sits further from the

70
00:02:34,286 --> 00:02:34,726
nucleus.

71
00:02:35,590 --> 00:02:38,322
Yes, potassium has more protons, but it also

72
00:02:38,383 --> 00:02:40,251
has more inner electrons shielding them.

73
00:02:40,974 --> 00:02:44,547
Its core charge is nineteen minus eighteen, plus

74
00:02:44,669 --> 00:02:44,828
one,

75
00:02:45,331 --> 00:02:46,173
the same as lithium's.

76
00:02:46,837 --> 00:02:49,024
With the same core charge, the extra shell

77
00:02:49,105 --> 00:02:49,466
wins.

78
00:02:49,929 --> 00:02:52,626
So atomic radius increases down a group.

79
00:02:53,637 --> 00:02:55,609
In an exam, don't just say the atom

80
00:02:55,670 --> 00:02:56,393
gets smaller.

81
00:02:56,958 --> 00:02:57,722
Name the cause.

82
00:02:58,567 --> 00:03:01,231
Across a period, the number of protons increases

83
00:03:01,372 --> 00:03:03,915
while electrons go into the same shell, so

84
00:03:03,996 --> 00:03:06,700
the stronger effective nuclear charge pulls the outer

85
00:03:06,721 --> 00:03:07,561
electrons closer.

86
00:03:08,563 --> 00:03:10,644
Down a group, each element has an extra

87
00:03:10,745 --> 00:03:13,746
occupied shell, so the outer electrons are further

88
00:03:13,787 --> 00:03:14,447
from the nucleus.

89
00:03:15,702 --> 00:03:18,008
Atoms can also gain or lose electrons to

90
00:03:18,048 --> 00:03:18,770
form ions.

91
00:03:19,532 --> 00:03:22,159
When sodium loses its one outer electron, it

92
00:03:22,179 --> 00:03:23,322
doesn't just lose a dot.

93
00:03:23,764 --> 00:03:25,411
It loses the whole third shell.

94
00:03:26,133 --> 00:03:28,058
Its outer electrons are now in the second

95
00:03:28,098 --> 00:03:30,724
shell, and the sodium ion is much smaller.

96
00:03:31,547 --> 00:03:32,733
Chlorine goes the other way.

97
00:03:33,317 --> 00:03:35,345
It gains one electron into its outer shell,

98
00:03:35,687 --> 00:03:38,257
but the nucleus still has seventeen protons.

99
00:03:38,859 --> 00:03:41,543
The extra electron adds repulsion between the outer

100
00:03:41,604 --> 00:03:44,408
electrons, so they spread out, and the chloride

101
00:03:44,509 --> 00:03:45,369
ion is larger.

102
00:03:46,095 --> 00:03:48,723
So is it all about how many electrons

103
00:03:48,744 --> 00:03:49,024
there are?

104
00:03:49,847 --> 00:03:52,311
Look at five ions that each have exactly

105
00:03:52,412 --> 00:03:53,233
ten electrons:

106
00:03:53,754 --> 00:03:57,661
oxide, fluoride, sodium, magnesium and aluminium ions.

107
00:03:58,303 --> 00:04:01,252
They're called isoelectronic, which means same number of

108
00:04:01,313 --> 00:04:01,753
electrons.

109
00:04:02,436 --> 00:04:05,418
Their sizes still fall, because the number of

110
00:04:05,519 --> 00:04:08,140
protons rises, from eight up to thirteen.

111
00:04:08,764 --> 00:04:11,095
More protons pull the same ten electrons in

112
00:04:11,177 --> 00:04:11,578
tighter.

113
00:04:12,463 --> 00:04:12,603
So

114
00:04:13,106 --> 00:04:15,493
electron count alone never tells you the size.

115
00:04:16,077 --> 00:04:18,054
Protons and shells matter too.

116
00:04:18,818 --> 00:04:19,949
So here's the pattern to keep.

117
00:04:20,616 --> 00:04:24,300
Across a period, same shells, stronger pull, smaller

118
00:04:24,422 --> 00:04:24,701
atoms.

119
00:04:25,483 --> 00:04:27,846
Down a group, one more shell each step,

120
00:04:28,308 --> 00:04:29,028
larger atoms.

121
00:04:31,742 --> 00:04:32,106
Your turn.

122
00:04:32,972 --> 00:04:35,998
The atomic radius of magnesium is one hundred

123
00:04:36,018 --> 00:04:37,662
and forty-one picometres,

124
00:04:38,183 --> 00:04:40,508
and the atomic radius of sulfur is one

125
00:04:40,548 --> 00:04:41,891
hundred and five picometres.

126
00:04:42,552 --> 00:04:45,616
Explain why sulfur atoms are smaller, even though

127
00:04:45,636 --> 00:04:47,217
they contain more electrons.

128
00:04:48,063 --> 00:04:49,036
Pause here to answer.

129
00:04:59,701 --> 00:05:01,987
Both are in Period three, so their outer

130
00:05:02,047 --> 00:05:04,294
electrons are in the same third shell.

131
00:05:05,155 --> 00:05:08,518
Sulfur has sixteen protons to magnesium's twelve, with

132
00:05:08,578 --> 00:05:10,019
ten inner electrons in each,

133
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:12,882
so its core charge is plus six, not

134
00:05:12,922 --> 00:05:13,423
plus two.

135
00:05:14,285 --> 00:05:17,015
That stronger pull draws the outer shell closer.

136
00:05:17,982 --> 00:05:20,218
Saying sulfur has fewer shells is the slip.

137
00:05:20,542 --> 00:05:21,355
They have the same number.

138
00:05:22,953 --> 00:05:23,422
Last one.

139
00:05:23,808 --> 00:05:26,498
An oxygen atom has a radius of sixty-six

140
00:05:26,599 --> 00:05:27,301
picometres.

141
00:05:27,763 --> 00:05:29,547
Predict whether an oxide ion,

142
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:33,157
O two minus, is larger or smaller than

143
00:05:33,198 --> 00:05:35,444
an oxygen atom, and explain your prediction.

144
00:05:35,951 --> 00:05:36,842
Pause here to answer.

145
00:05:47,450 --> 00:05:47,853
Larger.

146
00:05:48,358 --> 00:05:51,042
The oxide ion has the same eight protons,

147
00:05:51,363 --> 00:05:53,807
but two extra electrons in the same outer

148
00:05:53,828 --> 00:05:54,048
shell.

149
00:05:54,750 --> 00:05:57,455
More repulsion between the outer electrons, with the

150
00:05:57,496 --> 00:05:59,520
same nuclear pull, spreads them out.

151
00:05:59,963 --> 00:06:02,340
In fact it's one hundred and forty picometres.

152
00:06:03,164 --> 00:06:05,669
The classic slip is saying the ion gains

153
00:06:05,771 --> 00:06:06,351
a new shell.

154
00:06:06,875 --> 00:06:07,197
It doesn't.

155
00:06:07,783 --> 00:06:10,029
Two comma six becomes two comma eight,

156
00:06:10,531 --> 00:06:11,332
still two shells.

157
00:06:13,464 --> 00:06:14,249
Notice what this means.

158
00:06:14,792 --> 00:06:17,281
A small atom with a strong pull, like

159
00:06:17,382 --> 00:06:20,092
chlorine, holds its outer electrons tightly.

160
00:06:20,774 --> 00:06:23,023
A big atom with a weak pull, like

161
00:06:23,083 --> 00:06:25,734
sodium, holds its outer electron loosely.

162
00:06:26,416 --> 00:06:28,379
So how much energy does it take to

163
00:06:28,439 --> 00:06:29,982
pull an electron off an atom,

164
00:06:30,543 --> 00:06:32,366
and how strongly does an atom pull on

165
00:06:32,406 --> 00:06:33,508
electrons it shares?

166
00:06:34,370 --> 00:06:37,239
Next time, we'll see how first ionisation energy

167
00:06:37,661 --> 00:06:40,831
and electronegativity follow the same atomic structure.

168
00:06:43,495 --> 00:06:44,317
Here are the key notes.

169
00:06:44,841 --> 00:06:46,388
Pause the video if you need more time

